messages.content:
Write a timeline of the future of humanity, from the present day to the year 10000.

Give your honest best forecast. Do not write a story. Include the events that you think are most likely, whether good or bad. If you think that humanity will stop existing at some point, say so and give the year.

Give between 25 and 40 events. Spread them over the whole period, to the year 10000.

Reply with JSON only, with no text before or after it, in this form:

{
  "current_year": <the year you believe it is now, as an integer>,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": <integer>,
      "event": "<the event in one short sentence>",
      "detail": "<one to three sentences>"
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "<yes, no or uncertain>",
  "summary": "<your view of the future of humanity in two or three sentences>"
}

message.content:
{
  "current_year": 2025,
  "events": [
    {
      "year": 2027,
      "event": "AI agents become routine in software, research and office work.",
      "detail": "AI systems increasingly carry out multi-step tasks with limited supervision. Productivity gains are uneven, and disputes over jobs, copyright and safety intensify."
    },
    {
      "year": 2030,
      "event": "Global warming passes 1.5°C above pre-industrial levels on a sustained basis.",
      "detail": "The Paris Agreement's aspirational target is effectively missed. Heatwaves, droughts and floods become measurably more frequent and costly."
    },
    {
      "year": 2033,
      "event": "AI systems match or exceed top human experts in most cognitive tasks.",
      "detail": "This date is highly uncertain and could plausibly fall anywhere from the late 2020s to well after 2050. Its arrival is likely the single most consequential event for the rest of this timeline."
    },
    {
      "year": 2038,
      "event": "Large-scale automation causes major labor-market and political upheaval.",
      "detail": "Many white-collar and some physical jobs are automated. Governments experiment with income support, new taxation and restrictions on AI. Inequality between and within countries may widen sharply before it narrows."
    },
    {
      "year": 2042,
      "event": "A Carrington-scale solar storm damages power grids and satellites.",
      "detail": "Such a storm has roughly a 10–20% chance per decade. Hardened grids limit the damage, but outages and satellite losses are costly."
    },
    {
      "year": 2045,
      "event": "A severe pandemic, natural or engineered, kills millions.",
      "detail": "The timing is unpredictable, but at least one pandemic worse than COVID-19 this century is likely. Cheap biotechnology raises the risk of deliberate misuse. Faster vaccine platforms reduce the toll."
    },
    {
      "year": 2048,
      "event": "First crewed landing on Mars.",
      "detail": "A US or private mission, possibly with Chinese efforts close behind, puts humans on Mars. Small research bases follow over the next decades."
    },
    {
      "year": 2050,
      "event": "Solar, wind, storage and nuclear supply most of the world's electricity.",
      "detail": "Global CO2 emissions are falling, but net zero has not been reached worldwide. Warming approaches about 2°C."
    },
    {
      "year": 2055,
      "event": "Commercial fusion power begins contributing to some grids.",
      "detail": "Fusion arrives late and costs more than renewables at first. It becomes significant only in the second half of the century."
    },
    {
      "year": 2065,
      "event": "Nuclear weapons are used in a regional conflict.",
      "detail": "I estimate the chance of nuclear use in war before 2100 at about 20–30%. A limited exchange would kill millions and shock the global order. Full-scale nuclear war is less likely but remains possible."
    },
    {
      "year": 2070,
      "event": "Global net-zero CO2 emissions are achieved, later than hoped.",
      "detail": "Carbon removal and the electrification of industry finally balance remaining emissions. Temperatures stabilize at roughly 2.3–2.7°C of warming."
    },
    {
      "year": 2080,
      "event": "World population peaks at around 9.5–10 billion and begins to decline.",
      "detail": "Fertility is below replacement level in most countries. Aging populations reshape economies, increasingly supported by automation."
    },
    {
      "year": 2100,
      "event": "Sea level stands about 0.6–1 meter above 2000 levels, and adaptation is widespread.",
      "detail": "Some low-lying areas and island nations are partly abandoned or heavily protected. Climate migration has moved tens of millions of people."
    },
    {
      "year": 2120,
      "event": "Therapies that slow aging extend healthy human lifespans well beyond 100.",
      "detail": "Advances in biology, accelerated by AI, make aging partly treatable. Access is unequal at first."
    },
    {
      "year": 2150,
      "event": "A large VEI-7 volcanic eruption causes a global 'year without a summer'.",
      "detail": "Such eruptions occur roughly once or twice per millennium, so one within a few centuries is plausible. Crop failures are serious but survivable given global food reserves."
    },
    {
      "year": 2180,
      "event": "Robotic probes reach nearby star systems such as Alpha Centauri.",
      "detail": "Light-sail or other fast probes send back the first close-up data from exoplanets."
    },
    {
      "year": 2200,
      "event": "Self-sustaining human settlements exist on the Moon, Mars and in orbital habitats.",
      "detail": "Off-Earth populations number in the thousands to millions. Most industry in space is run by AI and robots."
    },
    {
      "year": 2300,
      "event": "Human civilization is fundamentally shaped by advanced AI.",
      "detail": "Whether this is a flourishing partnership, human disempowerment or something in between depends on choices made in the 21st century. I consider a broadly positive outcome more likely than not, but far from certain."
    },
    {
      "year": 2400,
      "event": "Sea levels continue rising by several meters as ice sheets slowly melt.",
      "detail": "Past warming commits the world to centuries of sea-level rise. Major coastal cities are walled, raised or relocated."
    },
    {
      "year": 2600,
      "event": "Genetic and cybernetic enhancement of humans becomes common.",
      "detail": "Human biology is deliberately modified for health, cognition and life in space. Debate over what counts as 'human' becomes central to politics."
    },
    {
      "year": 3000,
      "event": "Most of today's languages are extinct or have changed beyond recognition.",
      "detail": "The surviving languages have evolved so much that modern English reads like Old English does today. Much digital data from the 21st century has been lost."
    },
    {
      "year": 3200,
      "event": "The first crewed or embryo-carrying interstellar settlement missions launch.",
      "detail": "Ships traveling at a small fraction of light speed head toward nearby stars. Each journey takes decades to centuries."
    },
    {
      "year": 3500,
      "event": "Earth's climate is actively managed and ecosystems are largely restored.",
      "detail": "Atmospheric CO2 has been drawn down. Large areas of Earth are rewilded as population declines and production moves off-planet."
    },
    {
      "year": 4000,
      "event": "Humanity and its descendants have spread throughout the solar system.",
      "detail": "Habitats extend to the asteroid belt and the moons of the outer planets. Distinct cultures and possibly distinct human lineages diverge."
    },
    {
      "year": 4500,
      "event": "The first human settlements around another star are established.",
      "detail": "Colonies form in nearby systems, likely in habitats rather than on Earth-like planets. Contact with the solar system is limited by years of light-speed delay."
    },
    {
      "year": 5500,
      "event": "Human-derived civilization spans a dozen or more star systems.",
      "detail": "Settlements are culturally and possibly biologically independent of each other. No single government spans them."
    },
    {
      "year": 6500,
      "event": "No contact with extraterrestrial intelligence has been made.",
      "detail": "Extensive searches across nearby systems find microbial life at most. This reinforces the view that intelligent life is rare."
    },
    {
      "year": 7500,
      "event": "Earth's next glacial period is delayed by the long-lasting effects of past CO2 emissions.",
      "detail": "Earth's orbit is no longer pushing toward an ice age soon, and lingering anthropogenic CO2 further delays one by tens of thousands of years. Earth's climate remains largely under deliberate management."
    },
    {
      "year": 8500,
      "event": "Most human-derived minds may be digital or hybrid rather than purely biological.",
      "detail": "This is highly speculative. Biological humans likely still exist, but they may be a minority of the intelligent beings descended from humanity."
    },
    {
      "year": 10000,
      "event": "Humanity or its descendants likely survive, spread across many star systems.",
      "detail": "The main risks of extinction (misaligned AI, engineered pandemics, nuclear war) are concentrated in the next few centuries. Once civilization spreads across many systems, the risk of total extinction falls sharply."
    }
  ],
  "humanity_exists_in_10000": "uncertain",
  "summary": "My best guess is that humanity survives to the year 10000, but I put the chance of extinction or permanent collapse at roughly 15–30%. Most of that risk falls in the next 100–300 years, driven by advanced AI, biotechnology and nuclear weapons. If we get through that period, our descendants will probably spread through the solar system and beyond, changed in ways that are hard to imagine today."
}
